#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Privileged half of KSysSupervisor's disk utility.

Runs as root through pkexec and carries out the operations on a disk that
need it: formatting and relabelling, checking and repairing a filesystem,
TRIM, defragmenting and overwriting free space, copying a drive to another
or to and from an image file, editing the partition table (creating,
deleting, resizing and moving partitions) and reading a drive for files to
recover. It speaks one JSON object per
line on stdin/stdout so the unprivileged GUI can drive it across one
authorisation.

Three rules shape everything here.

Every argument is a claim to be checked. The client names a device; this
process decides for itself, from sysfs and /proc, whether that device exists,
whether it is still the one the user was looking at (names like sdd are handed
out again when a stick is swapped), whether the running system lives on it,
and whether anything has it open. A user interface bug must not be able to
reach a system volume, so the refusal is here and not only there.

The sequence belongs to this process, not to the client. The client asks for
"format this as exFAT called PHOTOS"; the steps and their order are decided
here, every tool the whole sequence needs is found before the first one runs,
and a step that fails stops the sequence. If the GUI dies halfway the sequence
still finishes, so a disk is never left with a partition table and no
filesystem because a window was closed.

The result is checked, not assumed. After the last step the device is read
again and compared with what was asked for; a tool exiting 0 is not the proof.

Deliberately standalone: nothing from the ksyssupervisor package and nothing
outside the standard library, because it runs as root outside the
application's virtualenv. External tools are run by absolute path with an
argument list - never through a shell.
"""

